**The common theorem can be stated without geometric vocabulary.** Let `π:D→Q` be the retained observation, with `Q=π(D)`, and `w:D→Y` the target. Exact recovery is possible precisely when

\[
\pi(x)=\pi(y)\Longrightarrow w(x)=w(y).
\]

Equivalently, `Eq(π)⊆Eq(w)`, or there is a unique `w̄:Q→Y` with `w=w̄∘π`. The proof defines `w̄` by choosing any representative of a fiber; the implication makes the choice immaterial. One validated conflicting pair refutes exact recovery. A clean sample does not prove recovery on an unexamined domain. The canonical information repair is the attained pair `(π,w)`, but the target may not be available to measure. This is explicitly proved in M4, lines 25–49 and 67–95, and encoded in GQG's quotient and context rules, M3, lines 44–192.

The word *kernel* needs care here. `Eq(π)` is a relation on pairs. In Hidden Quotient it is induced by a linear/algebraic kernel: two functions have the same multiplication operator when their difference lies in that kernel. In winding parity it is induced by the subgroup `2ℤ³`. The common abstraction is real; the ideal, subgroup, and arbitrary equivalence relation are not interchangeable structures.

**Hidden Quotient demonstrates both what quotienting accomplishes and what it cannot recover.** For actual bounded measurable functions,

\[
\pi(f)=M_f,\qquad
\ker\pi=\{f:f=0\text{ a.e. on every finite-measure measurable set}\}.
\]

Testing on characteristic functions of finite-measure sets proves one direction. For the converse, every square-integrable function is supported, up to null sets, on a countable union of finite-measure level sets. Hence a locally null multiplier annihilates every such test. The induced faithful C*-representation has

\[
\|M_f\|=\|[f]\|=\|f\|_{\infty,\mathrm{loc}}.
\]

This need not equal the ordinary essential supremum for an arbitrary measure. On `[0,1] ⊔ {p}`, with Lebesgue measure on the interval and infinite mass at `p`, `1_{p}` has nonzero ordinary essential supremum but acts as the zero multiplier. These arguments and the example are in M1, §§3–7.

For the target `w(f)=f(p)`, the old operator observation cannot recover the target: `f` and `f+1_p` have identical operators but different point values. Retaining actual point evaluation repairs this particular loss. It must be an additional observation on actual functions, not a function invented on their old equivalence classes. M2 explicitly works through this restoration at lines 484–525. Factoring out the invisible difference and measuring that difference answer different questions.

There is an important version correction. M1, line 325, states an overbroad Radon–Nikodym formulation. The qualified v1.7 source M2, lines 306–317, requires absolute continuity with respect to `λ=μ_sf` **and strong λ-semifiniteness** in its quantified density statement. Absolute continuity alone is not the full hypothesis. Semifinite reduction also does not supply missing order suprema; localizable completion is a separate construction. I rely on the corrected formulation, not the stronger wording in the older file. Some inline formulas are missing from the v1.7 text export, so the intact earlier Markdown is useful for the elementary kernel proof, while the explicit correction governs the later density claim. No operator-algebra or Radon–Nikodym theorem has been transferred to the toroidal or acquisition models merely because they also use quotients.

**GQG supplies a disciplined way to express these connections.** Its normative `Set` construction is

\[
\Omega_{\mathcal W}(x)=(w(x))_{w\in\mathcal W},\quad
x\sim_{\mathcal W}y\iff\Omega_{\mathcal W}(x)=\Omega_{\mathcal W}(y),\quad
D/{\sim_{\mathcal W}}\cong\operatorname{im}\Omega_{\mathcal W}.
\]

Retaining context `r` replaces the observation by `(Ω,r)` and refines the fibers. Conditioning on `r=s` instead restricts the source domain. Conditioning after quotient is defined only when `r` itself descends. M3 gives the concrete example: parity does not determine residue modulo four; retaining mod-four context refines the quotient, while restricting to `4ℤ` is a different operation. This is directly relevant to winding parity and to label-conditioned acquisition. [M3, lines 44–192, 353–383.]

The card calls itself an experimental language specification. It distinguishes the mathematical witness specification from code, parameters, seeds, and an execution manifest. A hash identifies bytes; it does not show that an implementation realizes the mathematical witness. Weighted marginalization and partition-function summation are explicitly outside the `Set` quotient semantics without a suitable backend or external theorem. T2 strengthens this discipline with relation types such as `DERIVED_DESCENT`, `COCYCLE_IDENTITY`, `CONSTRUCTOR_CHOICE`, and `COMPARED_WITH`. These are useful distinctions, not evidence that every proposed relation already has a proof. [M3, lines 5–41, 224–254, 302–313, 379–383; T2, lines 3–20.]

**The toroidal quotient is a genuine instance, with a genuine dynamical obstruction.** On the declared divergence-free, source-free domain, signed cut winding is `W∈ℤ³` and

\[
q=W\bmod2\in(\mathbb Z/2)^3.
\]

For every sector `q`, the currents built from reference cycles with winding `q+2k`, `k∈ℤ³`, give the same parity. Thus parity loses signed winding in every attained sector. `W` determines `q`, but `q` does not determine `W`. With charge-six sources, unrestricted signed integer cut winding is no longer the same valid observable; the source uses modular flux instead. These domain restrictions are part of the result. [T1, lines 1358–1402; T6, lines 22–28; T7, lines 487–514.]

The path increment and its accumulation obey

\[
\eta_n=(W_{n+1}-W_n)\bmod2=q_{n+1}-q_n,\qquad
c_q(m,n)=\sum_{k=m}^{n-1}\eta_k=q_n-q_m,
\]

\[
c_q(m,n)+c_q(n,p)=c_q(m,p).
\]

This is an exact cocycle identity and, more specifically, an endpoint-difference coboundary. Its additivity follows by telescoping. It does not establish a nontrivial cohomology class or a transition law computable from `q_n` alone. The separate throat quantity `c_F(a,b)=Q_c(b)-Q_c(a)` has the same additive form in a **spatial** coordinate; it is not the same observable, coefficient group, or dynamics. Periodic compensation can sum to zero while individual spatial increments are nonzero. [T1, lines 1404–1445, 1457–1546; T3, lines 463–474.]

This distinction is not merely cautionary. T3 gives an elementary Villain-sector counterexample. For current `2m` around a fixed reference loop of length `L`, every state has `q=0`. With `a=L/(2J)>0`, the next-sector toggle probability for `m≥1` is

\[
p_m=\tfrac12(1+e^{-(4m+1)a}).
\]

In particular `p_1>p_2`, so the next-sector law is not constant on a parity fiber. Using `m=1,2` avoids any accidental equality possible when comparing a special pair involving `m=0`. This proves non-descent for that elementary update. It does not by itself settle a different complete macro-sweep kernel. [T3, lines 477–514.]

The later consolidation contains a **positive repair theorem**, not just a suggested test. Put `A_α=Σ_{ℓ∈Γ_α}I_ℓ`. For the scheduled Villain attempt,

\[
p_\alpha(A)=\tfrac12\left[\min\{1,e^{-(L+2A)/(2J)}\}+\min\{1,e^{-(L-2A)/(2J)}\}\right].
\]

This is even in `A`. Consequently `(q,|A_α|)` determines the orientation-averaged next-sector law, and `(q,A_α)` determines the orientation-resolved acceptance quantities. This is an explicit connection between the toroidal calculation and witness-relative refinement. It proves sufficiency for this target and update; it does not prove minimal measurement cost or autonomous evolution of the enlarged state. [T7, lines 518–529.]

The separate TD-COS-FH-001 all-orders theorem concerns an ideal **unbounded-current cosine kernel**, at `J=1`, `t=1/2`, `h6=0`, fixed cubic `L≥2`, and fixed positive attempted-microtick spacing. Its argument goes beyond a one-step collision: finite stationary Markov order would force `A²u=cAu` for a killed-sector operator `A=DK^dD`; a nonzero tail correction contradicts that identity. The source also treats a fixed time-homogeneous finite-order law under other initial distributions. This is a supplied analytical theorem, not an inference from finite test counts. It is not an all-orders theorem for `(q,W)`, a truncated-current simulator, accepted-move sampling, adaptive clocks, or every finite-dimensional hidden-state representation. [T4, lines 6–27, 29–78 and subsequent proof; T6, lines 30–41; T7, lines 531–601.]

T7, lines 578–601, additionally gives a separately reviewed analytical extension to fixed rectangular side lengths `Lx,Ly,Lz≥2`, finite `J>0`, and `0<t<1`, with the same source-free unbounded cosine model and fixed attempted-microtick clock. It retains the negative leading correction needed for the proof. This extends the mathematical parameter scope; it does not change the named reference execution profile or establish implementation conformance.

**v2.6 changes the cost of obtaining sufficient information, not the criterion for sufficiency.** A finite primitive menu `c_j` gives each bad pair a separating set

\[
S_{xy}=\{j:c_j(x)\ne c_j(y)\},\qquad
\pi(x)=\pi(y),\ w(x)\ne w(y).
\]

A batch repairs the target exactly when it hits every such set. An empty separating set makes the target unreachable with that menu. Under the stated fixed nonnegative costs and independent choices across labels,

\[
C_{\mathrm{fixed}}=\tau_a(\cup_q H_q),\quad
C_{\mathrm{label,worst}}=\max_q\tau_a(H_q),\quad
C_{\mathrm{label,expected}}=\sum_qp(q)\tau_a(H_q).
\]

These are v2.5's one-batch-after-label formulas carried into v2.6. The initial label is available to both competitors, queries do not change the state, and exactness covers even attained zero-probability labels. [M4, lines 87–145.]

For v2.6's threshold family, `D={1,…,n}`, constant initial label, target `w(x)=x`, and unit-cost queries `c_t(x)=1[x≥t]`, every adjacent pair requires its own unique cut. A fixed batch must therefore use all `n−1` thresholds. Sequential binary search uses `ceil(log₂ n)` queries in the worst case, and the binary decision-tree leaf bound proves optimality:

\[
\frac{C_{\mathrm{fixed}}}{C_{\mathrm{sequential,worst}}}
=\frac{n-1}{\lceil\log_2n\rceil}\longrightarrow\infty.
\]

| States n | Exact batch queries | Optimal sequential worst case | Possible threshold inventory for either method |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 4 | 3 | 2 | 3 |
| 8 | 7 | 3 | 7 |
| 32 | 31 | 5 | 31 |
| 256 | 255 | 8 | 255 |

The sequential policy still needs access to every cut it might query. Thus per-case acquisition decreases while the necessary inventory does not. This is a valid elementary theorem built from hitting sets and binary search; the documents make no mathematical-priority claim. [M4, lines 129–145; M5, entire increment.]

A later source layer must also be retained in this comparison. T7, lines 776–785, states the general finite decision-tree recurrences, with a state prior for expected cost:

\[
V(S)=\min_j\{a_j+\max_vV(S_{jv})\},\quad
F(S)=\min_j\{a_jP(S)+\sum_vF(S_{jv})\}.
\]

Leaves have constant target; informative queries strictly shrink the candidate set. This is a natural sequential refinement of the same recovery problem. T7 reports a source solver checked on 2,348 small models, while explicitly saying that suite was not rerun in that consolidation. The recovered `geometry_adaptive.py` is instead the label-conditioned **batch** optimizer, as its own opening and M8 state. These must not be conflated. For the four-state prior `(0.7,0.1,0.1,0.1)`, optimal expected threshold cost is 1.5, but its tree has worst cost 3; a worst-optimal tree has cost 2. Optimizing the two objectives can select different policies.

An adaptive transcript, including the initial label and query identities/answers, is itself a retained observation. Exactness means its leaves are target-homogeneous. That is the precise GQG connection. Acquiring extra information about one unchanged state is not the same operation as predicting an evolving hidden state from a finite sector history. The v2.6 theorem therefore neither contradicts nor circumvents the toroidal all-orders obstruction.

